      <section id="introduction" class="section">
        <p>The <strong>DOT aerosol transport regulation</strong> framework ensures that pressurized gas containers are transported safely across domestic and international shipping corridors. It works by classifying aerosol products under hazardous material protocols based on container volume, propellant flammability, and structural pressure limits. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because failure to adhere to these rules results in severe compliance fines, rejected shipments, or catastrophic transport fire incidents. For a foundational exploration of container design limits and failure modes under thermal stress, refer to our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained" target="_top">aerosol can safety explained</a>.</p>
        <p>Aerosol shipping classifications require direct knowledge of chemical properties and containment specs, since any thermal instability can cause pressurisation issues in cargo holds. High-temperature conditions, especially during road haulage or air transit, can lead to package fatigue. Proper assessment is key to preventing containment failures and complying with dangerous goods laws.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="principles" class="section">
        <h2>Introduction to DOT & UN Aerosol Transport Regulations</h2>
        <p>Aerosol products are unique hazardous materials because they combine pressurized gases, flammable propellants, and active chemical concentrates within a single metallic container. During transport, these containers are exposed to mechanical vibrations, extreme temperatures, and ambient pressure fluctuations that can compromise container structural integrity. The Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration (PHMSA), a branch of the US Department of Transportation, enforces rules designed to prevent accidental product release during transit. These requirements are outlined in detail in the <a href="https://www.phmsa.dot.gov/hazmat/transporting-hazardous-materials" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PHMSA hazmat transportation guide</a>.</p>
        <p>To safely manage these risks, logistics professionals must verify that all aerosol packages comply with international dangerous goods harmonized frameworks. Compliance involves verifying container pressure limits, choosing matching valves and caps, and ensuring outer boxes can protect the cans. These standards prevent leakage and dangerous chemical reactions when packages are stacked in cargo trucks or shipping containers. To learn more about standard documentation requirements like Safety Data Sheets, read our technical walkthrough on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/understanding-sds-tds-and-coa-for-aerosol-products" target="_top">understanding SDS, TDS, and COA documents</a>.</p>
      </section>

