      <section id="levels" class="section">
        <h2>UN Aerosol Hazmat Classification Criteria</h2>
        <p>The international system for classifying dangerous goods is established by the United Nations Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods. This harmonized framework places all aerosols under Class 2 (Gases) and assigns them the unified shipping identification number UN 1950. Within this classification, products are sub-categorized based on propellant flammability, toxicity, and corrosive properties of the underlying formulation. The global guidelines for these hazards are outlined in the <a href="https://unece.org/dangerous-goods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UN Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods</a>.</p>
        <p>Classification determines whether a product is marked as Division 2.1 (Flammable Gases), Division 2.2 (Non-flammable, Non-toxic Gases), or Division 2.3 (Toxic Gases). Formulations are categorized as flammable under UN rules if they contain more than a nominal mass fraction of flammable components. If the formulation chemical heat of combustion exceeds a set threshold, it is automatically classified as a flammable aerosol. For a thorough breakdown of how labels must present these classifications to handlers, read our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/understanding-sds-tds-and-coa-for-aerosol-products" target="_top">GHS & HazCom labeling for aerosol products</a>.</p>

