        <h2>DOT 49 CFR Aerosol Shipping Rules & Limited Quantity Exemptions</h2>
        <p>Under US domestic law, dangerous goods shipping rules are codified in Title 49 of the Code of Federal Regulations. The specific regulations governing pressurized gas containers are detailed under 49 CFR Section 173.306, which provides rules for shipping limited quantities of compressed gases. This section allows certain packages to bypass full hazardous material shipping papers, placards, and certified UN containers if they meet strict safety criteria. The exact regulatory provisions are maintained in the federal <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eCFR Title 49 standard repository</a>.</p>
        <p>To qualify for the Limited Quantity exemption under DOT rules, the inner container capacity must not exceed 1.0 Liter (1,000 mL). The containers must be packed inside robust outer boxes, and the total gross weight of the package must not exceed 30 kilograms (66 pounds). These packages must be marked on the exterior with the black-and-white Limited Quantity diamond symbol. This marking notifies logistics workers that the packages contain small volumes of dangerous goods, allowing them to be transported by road or rail without formal hazardous material manifesting.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
