      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="compliance" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol Transport Packaging Regulation & Drop Test Standards</h2>
        <p>The primary goal of any aerosol transport packaging regulation is to prevent the accidental discharge of contents during shipping. When aerosol products are fully regulated (i.e. when they exceed the capacity or pressure thresholds for Limited Quantity exemptions), they must be packed in UN-certified specification outer boxes. These boxes must meet Performance Group II standards, which are verified through rigorous packaging testing protocols. These tests simulate transport stresses to confirm the boxes can protect the pressurized cans from damage.</p>
        <p>The performance tests require the loaded shipping container to pass a 1.2-meter (4-foot) drop test onto a solid concrete pad in five distinct orientations (flat on bottom, top, long side, short side, and corner). The packaging must also pass a stacking test, where a load equivalent to the weight of similar packages stacked to a height of 3 meters is applied for 24 hours. The inner containers must be packed with protective caps or recessed valves to prevent the valve from actuating if the box is dropped or compressed in transit.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Aerosol cans must be oriented vertically in the outer package with valves pointing upward. This configuration ensures that any pressure release involves vapor phase propellant rather than liquid phase concentrate, reducing potential hazard levels.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="chemistry" class="section">
        <h2>Dangerous Goods Aerosol Air Transport & IATA Compliance</h2>
        <p>Shipping aerosols by air introduces extra risks due to low cargo compartment temperatures, vibrations, and pressure drops at high altitudes. The International Air Transport Association publishes rules based on the International Civil Aviation Organization technical instructions to regulate air transport. These standards apply to all international flights and most domestic air cargo operators. The official requirements are detailed in the <a href="https://www.iata.org/en/programs/cargo/dgr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations portal</a>.</p>
        <p>Under air transport rules, Limited Quantity packages are subject to stricter packaging requirements than ground shipments. They must be able to withstand a differential pressure of at least 95 kPa (13.8 psi) without leaking, and they require a formal Limited Quantity marking that includes the letter "Y" in the center of the diamond. Furthermore, passenger aircraft ban Division 2.1 flammable aerosols in cargo holds, restricting them to cargo-only flights with lower total package weight limits. Non-flammable Division 2.2 aerosols are allowed on passenger aircraft but face strict net weight limits per package.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="design" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol IATA Shipping Regulation Checklist</h2>
        <p>To avoid transit delays, logistics operators should use a standardized checklist for air cargo shipments. Each package must have the correct hazard label (Division 2.1 or 2.2) and the UN 1950 shipping name marked on the box exterior. The shipper must also prepare a Dangerous Goods Declaration, listing the proper technical name, hazard division, packaging group, and emergency response contact details. This documentation must be signed by certified hazardous materials personnel who have completed mandated training.</p>
        <p>Air shipping requires double-walled corrugated fiberboard boxes (such as 4G UN specification boxes) with plastic interior liners to contain any accidental leaks. Valves must be protected by durable plastic caps that fit securely and do not pop off during vibration testing. Before shipping, logistics teams should verify that the carrier is authorized to handle the specific hazard division. Some cargo operators maintain custom restrictions that are stricter than general international rules.</p>
      </section>

