            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the primary regulation governing domestic aerosol transport in the US?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Domestic aerosol shipping within the United States is regulated by the Department of Transportation (DOT) under Title 49 of the Code of Federal Regulations (49 CFR), specifically Part 173.306 for Limited Quantity exceptions. This framework outlines the pressure thresholds, packaging configurations, and capacity limits required to transport pressurized gas containers legally and safely.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does the UN classify aerosol products for international transport?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Under the UN Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods Model Regulations, aerosols are classified under Class 2 (Gases) and assigned the identification number UN 1950. They are categorized based on their contents as flammable (Division 2.1), non-flammable/non-toxic (Division 2.2), or toxic (Division 2.3), which determines the corresponding handling and labeling protocols.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the requirements for shipping aerosols under the Limited Quantity exemption?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">To qualify for Limited Quantity exemptions, each inner aerosol container must have a capacity of 1,000 mL (1.0 L) or less. The containers must be packed in sturdy outer packages with a total gross package weight not exceeding 30 kg (66 lbs). The package must be marked with the standard Limited Quantity black-and-white diamond symbol, exempting it from full dangerous goods shipping documentation for domestic highway transport.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the hot water bath testing requirements for shipping aerosol cans?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The DOT 49 CFR and UN model regulations require that every filled aerosol container undergo a hot water bath test before shipment. The water bath must be maintained at 55 °C (131 °F), or 50 °C (122 °F) if the liquid phase does not exceed 95% of the capacity at that temperature. This thermal exposure raises internal pressures to ensure the container structural integrity is verified and any potential leakage is detected.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Can flammable aerosols be shipped by air under IATA regulations?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, flammable aerosols (UN 1950, Division 2.1) can be shipped by air, but they are subject to strict quantity limitations and packaging requirements. Under IATA Packaging Instruction 203, passenger aircraft shipping is highly restricted (typically limited to 75 kg net weight per package or forbidden for certain flammable formulations), while cargo aircraft allow larger volumes up to 150 kg per package when enclosed in certified UN specification outer packaging.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What packaging group performance level must fully regulated aerosols meet?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Fully regulated aerosol products that do not qualify for or use the Limited Quantity exemption must be shipped in UN-certified specification outer packagings. These packagings must meet the Performance Group II standards. This involves rigorous verification testing including a 1.2-meter drop test, structural stack testing, and compliance with specific interior liner or padding specifications to prevent accidental discharge.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

